{"author":"Rebar Bot Nava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"description":"## Problem\n\nsmiling-ancient-finwhale (2e85d8558a4) heals a reclaim-superseded clone on `append_event`, `transition_core` and `claim_core` (precheck raises `SupersededStoreRefused`; heal with the lock free; retry once). These still refuse such a clone until a read or idle-sync round moves it:\n\n- `rebar delete` (`_commands/delete.py::delete_cli`)\n- NDJSON import (`_io/import_ndjson.py::import_tickets`; `_flush` calls `_seam._refuse_unpublishable_store` directly)\n- a reconciler pass (`reconciler/inbound_translate.py::_write_event_file` → `finalize_event`)\n- claim or start work with the plan-review gate on: `gates.plan_review_precheck` and `claim --review`'s `claim._review_before_claim` call `claim_gate_check`, whose `freshness.store_freshness` reads a superseded clone as `diverged` and returns `stale-store`\n- a completion close when `sync.pull` is off: its only heal is `transition_close._refresh_close_material` → `ensure_fresh(force=True)`, which returns early under `sync.pull=off`, so the completion close gate refuses with `stale-store`.\n\n## Cause (reproduced)\n\nNone of these calls the heal. Reproduced at 2e85d8558a4 (per-path output in the ticket comments). Not affected (tests pass there): a completion close with `sync.pull` on; review-plan with `sync.pull` on or off, because its repo snapshot (`_snapshot/repo_snapshot.py::_pin_tickets_sha`, `fetch=True`) calls `sync.reconverge` directly, not through `ensure_fresh`, before `_stale_store_verdict`.\n\n## Approach\n\n- **One heal entry.** `freshness.heal_superseded_clone(tracker, *, lock_timeout=...)` gains an optional lock budget (default unchanged). New `freshness.heal_if_superseded(tracker, repo_root) -> bool` probes `write_blocking_divergence` (local git only); only on a proven supersession it calls `heal_superseded_clone` with the read path's named short budget (`reads._RECONVERGE_LOCK_TIMEOUT`), re-probes and returns the remaining write-blocking divergence (or `None`); if one remains it logs a WARNING naming the remote ref and that the condition is transient (fetch failure or busy lock). Never raises.\n- **delete — heal-and-retry.** `delete_cli` holds no write lock and rolls back every file it wrote on `CommandError`. Move resolve-through-commit into one function wrapped in `_seam.retry_after_superseded_heal`; the rollback re-raises `SupersededStoreRefused`, so resolution and the child check are redone on the healed store.\n- **NDJSON import — heal before its reads, heal-and-retry at flush.** Except for `--dry-run`, `import_tickets` calls `heal_if_superseded` before `_scan_existing_source_ids`, so its id map, dedupe scan and buffered events are built on the healed store. `_flush` runs its precheck through `retry_after_superseded_heal`, matching the import's other mid-run writes, which already heal through the wrapped `append_event`/`transition_core` and the close refresh. A mid-run heal keeps the import's commits: the override replays fresh unpushed events byte-identically; only a clone already holding an unpushed event older than the reclaim horizon replays nothing (ADR 0106 §5's accepted loss, kept under the backup ref).\n- **reconciler pass — heal before, abort if unhealed.** A mid-pass heal is unsafe: by the first inbound write the pass has made outbound Jira mutations (so it cannot be retried) and holds uncommitted inbound event files and binding-state edits until `pass_support._commit_binding_store_snapshot`; the override's `reset --hard` would discard tracked edits. So `guarded_pass.run_pass_result` calls `heal_if_superseded` before `reconcile_once` (skipped for the read-only `preview` route) and, if a write-blocking divergence remains, returns without calling `reconcile_once`, so no Jira mutation precedes a refused write: RESCHEDULE for an unhealed supersession (transient: fetch failure or busy lock), OPERATIONAL_FAILURE naming DIVERGED for an unrelated remote (permanent). A reclaim fetched mid-pass is still refused by the unchanged precheck.\n- **gated claim / start work — heal before the gate.** `gates.plan_review_precheck` and `claim --review`'s `claim._review_before_claim` call `heal_if_superseded` first. Both run before `claim_core`/`transition_core` take the lock, and `claim --review` no longer reads `stale-store` and pays for a needless review.\n- **completion close — heal in the pre-gate refresh.** `_refresh_close_material` (unforced closes only; before the open-child scan, state reduction and both close gates; outside the lock) also calls `heal_if_superseded`, independent of `sync.pull`. The verifier then reads the healed store and its receipt pins the healed tickets OID.\n- **Unchanged:** `store_freshness`, `claim_gate_check` and the read-only callers (`review-plan --status`, MCP `plan_review_status`, plan-review reuse) never run the heal; a `behind` store is still not adopted; review-plan; `completion_txn`, whose receipt check (`validate_receipt` → `non_ancestor_tickets_history`) makes retry-after-heal impossible by design.\n- `docs/concurrency.md`: list which writers heal-and-retry, which heal before they start and why, and that read-only gate queries never heal.\n- No new config key, env var or flag.\n\n## Scope\n\nIn: the files in the recorded file impact (freshness, delete, import, guarded_pass, gates, claim, transition_close, docs/concurrency.md, the shared `reclaim_harness.py`, and both reclaim test modules). Out: a clone whose remote ref has not been fetched yet.\n\n## Testing\n\nNew module, shared harness:\n- superseded clone: delete, import, reconciler pass, gated claim, gated start-work transition, and a `sync.pull=off` completion close (verifier stubbed) succeed on the reclaimed baseline (gated claim/start fail only for the missing attestation). Fail on 2e85d8558a4.\n- unrelated remote (lineage names another epoch): delete, import, reconciler pass, gated claim and the completion close gate are refused (DIVERGED; the gates via `stale-store` naming DIVERGED); clone, working tree and remote unchanged.\n- unchanged paths: `sync.pull=on` close and review-plan (both `sync.pull` values) reach their stubbed step healed; `claim_gate_check` on a superseded clone still reports `stale-store` and moves nothing.\n- `claim --review` on a superseded clone: its own `claim_gate_check` no longer reports `stale-store` (review stubbed).\n- heal failure: fetch failing, for comment, delete, import, reconciler pass, gated claim and `sync.pull=off` close; write lock held by another holder, for import, reconciler pass, gated claim and close (the short-budget sites). Each refusal names DIVERGED (gates via `stale-store`; the reconciler as a RESCHEDULE without calling `reconcile_once`; an unrelated remote as OPERATIONAL_FAILURE without calling it); HEAD, working tree, an unpushed local event and the remote unchanged; no stale-override backup ref.\n\nExisting related suites stay green, then `make verify`.\n\n## Acceptance criteria\n\n- [ ] On a clone a reclaim superseded (remote lineage names the local epoch, remote ref already fetched), `rebar delete`, an NDJSON import and a reconciler pass each succeed and write on top of the reclaimed baseline; regression tests prove each and fail on 2e85d8558a4.\n- [ ] On such a clone the completion-close store-freshness gate and the plan-review claim gate move the clone and do not report `stale-store`; regression tests prove both and fail on 2e85d8558a4. The atomic completion commit itself is left unchanged for the receipt-ancestry reason recorded in the Approach.\n- [ ] Against a remote whose lineage does not name the local epoch, delete, import, the reconciler pass and the completion-close gate are still refused as DIVERGED and write nothing.\n- [ ] A heal that fails (failed fetch; write lock held by another holder) leaves the write refused as DIVERGED and loses no event: HEAD, the working tree, an unpushed local event and the remote are unchanged, and no stale-override backup ref is created.\n- [ ] `make verify` passes."}},"env_id":"492a5c71-6cda-48a6-8134-02ff6a672739","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1795259360624909001,"uuid":"32aab3fd-0f5a-4b2d-bff8-5aa628db9fcc"}